## Why This Is Asked
Tests runtime extensibility across sinks and formats without touching existing call sites.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for serialization
- Registry for runtime extensibility
- Generic interfaces and type-safe dispatch

## Code Example
```typescript
export interface Serializer<T> {
  serialize(input: T): string
}

export class SerializerRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, Serializer<any>>()

  register(name: string, serializer: Serializer<any>): void {
    this.map.set(name, serializer)
  }

  get(name: string): Serializer<any> {
    const serializer = this.map.get(name)
    if (!serializer) {
      throw new Error(`Serializer '${name}' not found`)
    }
    return serializer
  }
}

// Usage example
const registry = new SerializerRegistry()
registry.register('json', new JsonSerializer())
registry.register('protobuf', new ProtobufSerializer())
registry.register('avro', new AvroSerializer())

const serializer = registry.get('json')
const output = serializer.serialize(data)
```

## Why This Is Asked
High-throughput gateways must support evolving protocols without touching core logic. A pluggable adapter layer provides clean separation and runtime extensibility.

## Key Concepts
- Adapter pattern for protocol translation
- Runtime registry for plugin adapters
- Low-allocation, fast dispatch to appropriate adapter
- Clear interfaces to avoid gateway coupling

## Code Example
```javascript
interface ProtocolAdapter {
  canHandle(req: Request): boolean;
  translate(req: Request): InternalRequest;
}
class ProtocolRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, ProtocolAdapter>();
  register(name: string, a: ProtocolAdapter): void { this.map.set(name, a); }
  translate(req: Request): InternalRequest {
    const a = this.map.get(req.protocol);
    if (!a) throw new Error('Unsupported protocol');
    return a.translate(req);
  }
}
```

